{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788301238,"nonce":"KTaYK/tmzvtDZnSDiOE8AW8Bw/I=","message":"PART 3 IS NOW BUILT, on `722c951`'s machinery as comment #2 said it should be.\nNOT PROVEN ON A BENCH. Branch `spec/advance-halt`, commits `f9f61d2` (source),\n`71fc15f` (acceptance), `c32edb8` (DESIGN). `dotnet build -c Release`: 0\nwarnings, 0 errors. The full write-up is on `722c951`; this is the part that\nbelongs here.\n\n## IT CONSUMES `triage`. There is no second path.\n\nComment #2 said \"`triage` publishes both numbers and the verdict, so the refusal\nhas everything it needs to consume. Do not grow a second refusal path here.\" So\n`advance` grew none.\n\n`Triage`'s per-patient body is extracted into `PawnActs.CasualtyRow(...)`, and\nBOTH callers use it: the `triage` verb and the new\n`PawnActs.BleedoutDeadline(map)` that `TimeDriver.Start` calls. One pathfind, one\ngate set, one comparison, one verdict string. The refusal reads\n`verdict == \"too-slow\"` off a row `triage` itself would publish and recomputes\nnothing — `bleedout_ticks` comes from `clock.ticks`, `margin_ticks` from\n`margin_ticks`, and `rescue_ticks` is derived from those two rather than measured\nagain. `PawnSerializer.BleedoutBlock` remains the single clock builder, so the\nclock a `pawn` read shows, the clock `triage` judges against and the clock the\nrefusal quotes are one number. `IsCasualty(...)` is also extracted, so the\nthree-way casualty union (downed OR needs-tend OR bleeding, and the\n`Alert_ColonistNeedsTend` inversion behind it) is stated once.\n\n`error.code: \"bleedout-deadline\"`, `ok:false`. A failed envelope carries no\n`data` (`Poller.BuildResultJson`), so the numbers are `key=value` tokens in\n`error.detail`: `bleedout_ticks=`, `rescue_ticks=`, `margin_ticks=`, `pawn=`,\n`pawn_name=`, `rescuer=`, `verdict=too-slow`. The sentence names the pawn, points\nat `rescue`, quotes the M1 tick-231,968 decision, and says the travel estimate is\na FLOOR so the real margin is worse than printed, not better.\n\n## THE DECISION COMMENT #2 DID NOT HAVE TO MAKE, AND I DID\n\n**It refuses on `too-slow` AND ON NOTHING ELSE.** `no-rescuer`, `no-path` and\n`no-deadline` do not refuse.\n\nThe refusal exists to force ONE decision into the open — \"this pawn dies unless\nyou act, and advancing is choosing not to\". That decision only exists when there\nis a TIMING question: a rescuer who could make it against a clock that says they\ncannot. Where nobody can reach the patient AT ALL, advancing changes nothing\nabout the outcome, no act clears the condition, the next advance would refuse\nidentically, and a ten-day unattended run stops dead on a state it cannot fix.\nThat is a wall, not a guard.\n\n**This is not hypothetical, and the orchestrator's bench pass is the evidence**\n(`accept/runs/s21-20260901/`): on a bare `--quicktest` map there is no bed\nanywhere, so `TakeToBedGate` → `HealthAIUtility.CanRescueNow` →\n`WantsToBeRescued` answers `no-bed` for every colonist and EVERY `triage` verdict\nis `no-rescuer` with a null `margin_ticks` and no `act`. A refusal keyed on\n\"somebody is bleeding\" rather than on the comparison would have made `advance`\nunusable on the standard bench.\n\nThe casualty is not lost by staying quiet here. `advance` HALTED on the downing\nwhen it happened (`722c951` #1). `digest.work_coverage` and\n`pawn.health.ticks_until_bleedout` carry it at every glance. And `triage` names\nthe gate that refused each candidate — `no-bed` says BUILD A BED, which is an\nact, where \"refuse to advance\" says nothing at all.\n\n`WantsToBeRescued`'s first two clauses (`!pawn.Downed`, `pawn.InBed()`) also put\na STANDING bleeder and a BEDDED bleeder outside this refusal, correctly: neither\nneeds carrying. A patient already in bed whose DOCTOR cannot arrive in time is a\ndifferent comparison — tend travel, not rescue travel — and I do not claim it.\nThat is the one boundary of part 3 I am leaving open, stated rather than hidden.\n\n## COST, AND WHEN IT IS SKIPPED\n\nSession 19's predicate-cost decision exists to stop a pathfinder running at every\nglance, so the gate is steep and checked cheapest-first. It runs ONCE at ARM on\n`advance` — never per frame, never per tick; `TimeDriver.Step` polls\n`WindowsForcePause` and the state predicate, and a pathfinder has no business in\neither.\n\n1. One `FreeColonistsSpawned` snapshot (the cached list rebuilds on every\n   access).\n2. Per colonist, **ONE CACHED FLOAT**: `Verse/HediffSet.BleedRateTotal` returns\n   `cachedBleedRate` and recomputes only when the game dirtied it. **Nobody\n   bleeding — the overwhelmingly common case — costs exactly this and stops\n   here.** No pathfind, no hediff walk, no `ShouldBeTendedNowByPlayer`.\n3. Only for a bleeder: the clock\n   (`HealthUtility.TicksUntilDeathDueToBloodLoss`, one division) and, only if it\n   is FINITE, the full row — which is where the `FindPathNow` calls live, capped\n   at `path_candidates` (3) per patient.\n\nWorst case: **3 pathfinds per BLEEDING patient, once per advance.** A colony with\nnobody bleeding pays one float per colonist.\n\n## ACCEPTANCE\n\n`accept/722c951-advance-halt.py` phase 6, and it is GATED ON `triage`'S OWN\nVERDICT rather than on my arithmetic. It downs a colonist with bleeding, then\nescalates `BloodLoss` severity (0.5 → 0.98), re-reading `triage` after each step,\nuntil the verdict is `too-slow`. Then it asserts the refusal reports `triage`'s\nnumbers verbatim (6.3–6.5), names the pawn (6.6), points at `rescue` (6.7),\ncarries no `data` block (6.8), and that `through_casualties` overrides it too\n(6.9–6.12).\n\nIf the escalation cannot reach `too-slow`, the suite exits 2 as a FIXTURE GAP and\nnames both known causes in the SKIP text: **no bed on a `--quicktest` map** (every\nverdict `no-rescuer`) and **a map with no distance in it** (the clock cannot be\ndriven below the walk without killing the patient). It does not pass a check it\ndid not earn. Every pre-escalation verdict is itself a live test of the negative\nhalf — none of them refused, or 6.2 would have fired before the escalation ran.\n\n## STILL OWED ON THIS ISSUE, and NOT mine\n\nComment #2's \"Owed\" list is otherwise untouched by this branch:\n\n- **The acceptance for parts 1, 2 and 2b** — `digest.work_coverage`,\n  `work-cover`, `triage` itself. Nothing of mine covers them; my phase 6 reads\n  `triage` as a fixture oracle, which is not the same as proving `triage`.\n- **`checklists/` colony-start item 7 shrinking to \"confirm the mod's read\", and\n  `[[one-doctor-is-zero-doctors]]` keeping only the WHY** — still an open\n  acceptance bullet. I added a `casualty-halt` item to `checklists/triggered.md`\n  (read `halted_on`, then `triage`, then send its `act`) and did not touch item 7,\n  since it is part 1's bullet and not part 3's.\n\n## BLOCKED / CAVEATS\n\n- Nothing has met a bench. I launched nothing; the orchestrator owns every\n  in-game acceptance.\n- The extraction of `CasualtyRow`/`IsCasualty` changes no published field of\n  `triage` — same keys, same order, same values. It is a refactor in service of\n  \"do not grow a second path\", and if worker A's `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py`\n  asserts on `triage`'s shape, that shape is unchanged.","files":null}]}